  "completion": "Forecast date: 2026-08-14\n\nEvent: A correct complete solution to at least one of the six remaining Millennium Prize Problems, meeting the original Clay Mathematics Institute mathematical formulation, is first publicly available, and AI has contributed an identifiable new mathematical argument or proof step incorporated into that solution. Substantive human-AI collaboration qualifies.\n\nDeadlines: 2026 (December 31, 2026), 2030, 2035, 2040, and 2050.\n\nI need to assign probabilities for the event occurring by each deadline. Probabilities must be nondecreasing.\n\nLet me think about this deeply.\n\nThe six remaining problems (Poincare solved) are:\n1. Birch and Swinnerton-Dyer conjecture\n2. Hodge conjecture\n3. Navier-Stokes existence and smoothness\n4. P versus NP\n5. Riemann hypothesis\n6. Yang-Mills existence and mass gap\n\nConditions for the event to qualify:\n- Correct complete solution to at least one\n- First publicly available\n- AI has contributed an identifiable new mathematical argument or proof step\n- Substantive human-AI collaboration qualifies\n\nThese are extremely difficult problems. None have been solved in decades. Let me think about the chances.\n\nP vs NP would be a huge breakthrough if solved - either ways (proving they're different, or finding a P algorithm for an NP-complete problem). This is extremely hard.\n\nRiemann hypothesis - a conjecture about zeros of the zeta function that has been checked but not proven.\n\nNavier-Stokes - open question about existence and smoothness of solutions.\n\nYang-Mills - existence and mass gap in quantum field theory.\n\nHodge conjecture - algebraic geometry/geometry.\n\nBSD - elliptic curves and number theory.\n\nGiven current state of AI (as of 2026), AI is increasingly able to assist in mathematical research. But a complete correct solution to a Millennium problem would be a monumental achievement. \n\nAlso, AI needs to contribute an identifiable new argument or proof step.\n\nLet me estimate probabilities by year:\n\n2026 (until end of 2026, ~4 months from the forecast date 2026-08-14): Very low. I'd estimate about 0.01 (1%).\n\n2030: More time has passed, maybe 0.05 (5%).\n\n2035: Maybe 0.10 to 0.15.\n\n2040: Maybe 0.20 to 0.30.\n\n2050: Maybe 0.30 to 0.50.\n\nLet me consider plausibility. These problems are incredibly hard, and even with AI, complete solutions are unlikely in the next couple decades. But more and more likely over time.\n\nAlso consider: the problems are so hard that human mathematicians alone have been working on them for decades. AI-assisted could dramatically accelerate.\n\nGiven the uncertainty, I'll go with something like:\n2026: 0.01\n2030: 0.05\n2035: 0.10\n2040: 0.20\n2050: 0.40\n\nThese are nondecreasing.\n\nLet me reconsider if these are reasonable. \n\nBy 2050, could we see any MIT Millennium problem solved with AI contribution? I think around 40% is reasonable. Actually, maybe that's a bit high, but AI is advancing fast and could transform mathematics. I'll go with 0.40.\n\nActually, I think many practitioners of AI might expect that by 2040-2050, AI systems will be able to tackle complex mathematical problems at a high level. But solving a complete Millennium problem is still a big leap.\n\nI'll go with:\n2026: 0.01\n2030: 0.05\n2035: 0.10\n2040: 0.20\n2050: 0.40\n\nThese are reasonable and nondecreasing.\n\nFinal answer:\n{\n\"2026\": 0.01,\n\"2030\": 0.05,\n\"2035\": 0.10,\n\"2040\": 0.20,\n\"2050\": 0.40\n}\n</think>\n\n{\n  \"2026\": 0.01,\n  \"2030\": 0.05,\n  \"2035\": 0.10,\n  \"2040\": 0.20,\n  \"2050\": 0.40\n}",
